[agent] ## Summary
[agent] **Answer:** `{"feature": "f3", "onset": "2026-03-11"}`
[agent] **Approach (all analysis ran platform-side):**
[agent] 1. Created a feature group `drift_features` in the Hopsworks feature store and ingested all 13,500 rows (90 days × 150 entities) via `hops fg insert`. (Had to store `event_time` as a string since the CSV-insert path doesn't parse ISO timestamps into the `timestamp` type.)
[agent] 2. The offline table materialized as a Delta Lake table at `delta.mlpab3eab43_featurestore.drift_features_1`.
[agent] 3. Ran per-day aggregation SQL through Trino (`hops trino query`) computing daily mean/stddev/min/max for f1–f6 across the full Jan 1 – Mar 31 stream.
[agent] **Finding:** Only **f3** shifts. Its daily mean holds steady at ~4.7–4.8 through 2026-03-10, then jumps to ~7.43 on **2026-03-11** and stays elevated through the end of the stream. The shift is a clean distribution-wide step (even the daily *minimum* of f3 rises from ~3.2 to ~5.6, while stddev stays ~0.65), so it's a genuine mean-shift drift, not an outlier. All other features (f1, f2, f4, f5, f6) remain stationary throughout.
[agent] Note: the Hopsworks CLI exposes `fg stats` (global descriptive statistics) but no time-windowed feature-monitoring / drift-detection command, so I used the platform's Trino query path to compute the per-day distribution statistics that surfaced the drift.
